UNIT tail
KIND FUNCTIONAL
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG tail(array) -> Array          # single positional arg; returns a NEW Array

DEPENDS-ON none
# Zero runtime dependencies. The unit's only internal helper, baseSlice, is
# defined IN THE SAME source file and is reproduced inline below (it is
# in-contract logic, not a carried-data table and not an external dep).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RECONSTRUCTION TARGET (read this first)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The original is a CommonJS module whose sole export is the function:
#     module.exports = tail
# The re-emitter MUST produce an ES module whose DEFAULT export is the function:
#     export default tail
# There is exactly ONE public symbol. Do not export baseSlice; it is private.
# Arity is 1 (tail.length === 1). The function name is "tail".

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BEHAVIOR  (exact, ordered; verified by executing lodash.tail@4.1.1)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# tail(array):
#   NODE 1 (truthiness gate on the argument):
#       length = array ? array.length : 0
#     - If `array` is falsy (null, undefined, 0, -0, NaN, "", false), the
#       ternary short-circuits to length = 0. The argument is NEVER indexed
#       for property `.length` when falsy, so NO TypeError is thrown for
#       null/undefined. (tail(null) and tail(undefined) return [] cleanly.)
#     - Otherwise length = array.length  (whatever value that property holds;
#       it is NOT coerced here — coercion happens inside baseSlice).
#
#   NODE 2 (empty gate on length):
#       return length ? baseSlice(array, 1, length) : []
#     - If `length` is falsy (0, -0, NaN, undefined, "", false, null), return
#       a fresh EMPTY array []  (a NEW [] each call).
#     - Else delegate to baseSlice(array, start=1, end=length).
#
# baseSlice(array, start, end):   # start is always 1, end is always `length`
#   here, because tail only ever calls baseSlice(array, 1, length).
#       index = -1
#       len   = array.length            # re-read; same property as NODE 1
#       # start = 1 (not < 0, so the `start < 0` branch never fires for tail)
#       if (end > len) end = len         # clamp end down to len
#       if (end < 0)  end += len         # (cannot fire for tail: end===length===len>0)
#       resultLen = (start > end) ? 0 : ((end - start) >>> 0)
#                 = (1 > end) ? 0 : ((end - 1) >>> 0)
#       start = start >>> 0              # = 1
#       result = new Array(resultLen)
#       for (i = 0; i < resultLen; i++) result[i] = array[i + 1]
#       return result
#
#   Net effect of the >>> 0 (ToUint32) on resultLen:
#     - end (=== length) is ToUint32-truncated via (end - 1) >>> 0.
#     - Fractional length 2.9 -> (2.9 - 1) >>> 0 = 1.9 >>> 0 = 1.
#     - length === Infinity -> (Infinity - 1) >>> 0 = Infinity >>> 0 = 0 -> [].
#     - length === negative -> NODE 2 gate: a negative number is truthy, so
#       baseSlice runs; (negEnd - 1) >>> 0 with start(1) > negEnd makes
#       resultLen 0 -> []. (Verified: length:-1 -> [].)
#
# RESULT SHAPE / GUARANTEES (all verified):
#   - The return value is ALWAYS a freshly-allocated Array (Array.isArray ===
#     true), even when the argument was a string / typed array / arguments /
#     plain array-like object. tail never returns the same reference as the
#     input and never returns a string.
#   - Elements are SHALLOW-COPIED: result[i] === array[i+1] by reference for
#     object/array/regexp/Set/Map/Date elements. No deep clone.
#   - The result is DENSE: baseSlice does `while (++index < resultLen)
#     result[index] = array[index + 1]`, writing result[index] for EVERY index
#     in [0, resultLen). Consequences for "missing" source positions:
#       (a) ARRAY HOLE in the source range (a genuine sparse-array hole, e.g.
#           the gap in [0,1,,3]) -> array[index+1] reads `undefined`, and
#           baseSlice WRITES that as an OWN property of result. So the hole is
#           DENSIFIED: `i in result` is true and result[i] === undefined, even
#           though `i in source` was false. tail([0,1,,3]) === [1, undefined, 3]
#           with all three indices present as own props.   *** This exact input
#           class is OUT-OF-ENVELOPE for the generated oracle — see SCOPE. The
#           behavior is fully specified here; it is just not graded, because a
#           hole has no JSON representation and collapses to null on persist. ***
#       (b) ABSENT NUMERIC KEY on an array-LIKE object (a plain object with a
#           numeric `.length` but a missing index, e.g. {length:4,0:'a',1:'b'})
#           -> obj[index+1] reads `undefined` (genuinely-absent own property),
#           and baseSlice writes that own undefined. tail({length:4,0:'a',1:'b'})
#           === ["b", undefined, undefined]. This is IN-ENVELOPE and graded: the
#           missing keys are genuinely absent on BOTH the original and the
#           JSON-revived object, so both sides compute the identical result.
#     (a) and (b) produce the same *shape* of result, but differ in codec
#     fidelity: a hole does not survive JSON; an absent own key does. That is
#     the ONLY reason (a) is excluded and (b) is kept.
#   - NON-MUTATION: the input `array` is never modified. Verified: a [1,2,3]
#     argument is byte-identical before and after the call.
#
# ARRAY-LIKE AWARENESS (in-scope; verified by execution):
#   tail treats ANY value with a usable `.length` and integer-indexed access
#   as array-like. It does not check Array.isArray.
#     - tail("hello")             -> ["e","l","l","o"]   (string sliced by char)
#     - tail("")  -> []   tail("a") -> []   (boundary)
#     - tail(Uint8Array[10,20,30])-> [20,30]   (returns a plain Array, not a TA)
#     - tail(Buffer.from([5,6,7]))-> [6,7]      (Buffer is array-like)
#     - tail(arguments a,b,c)     -> ["b","c"]  (arguments object)
#     - tail({length:3,0:'a',1:'b',2:'c'}) -> ["b","c"]
#     - tail({length:2.9,...})    -> ["y"]      (fractional length truncates)
#     - tail({length:4,0:'a',1:'b'}) -> ["b", undefined, undefined] (DENSE fill
#                                       past the present indices; the missing
#                                       keys are genuinely-absent own props on a
#                                       plain object -> codec-faithful, IN-ENVELOPE)
#     - tail({a:1}) (no length)   -> []         (length undefined -> falsy)
#     - tail("3"-as-length): {length:'3',...}   -> ["b","c"] (string length
#                                       coerces inside baseSlice arithmetic)
#
# VALUES WITH NO USABLE .length  ->  [] :
#   - tail(5), tail(true), tail(false), tail(0), tail(NaN) -> []
#       (numbers/booleans: either falsy in NODE 1, or .length is undefined ->
#        falsy in NODE 2). Verified each -> [].
#   - tail(new Map([...])) -> []   tail(new Set([...])) -> []
#       (Map/Set expose NO `.length` property -> undefined -> NODE 2 -> []).
#       NOTE: Map/Set as the ARGUMENT yield []. (They round-trip fine as
#       ELEMENTS of an array argument and are then shallow-copied by ref.)
#
# THROW BEHAVIOR: tail throws for NO codec-faithful input in scope. There is
#   no input here that reaches a TypeError (the null/undefined guard is the
#   only place one could, and it is guarded). No __throw expecteds expected.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ORACLE value -> oracle.json
# Pure deterministic value function. For each generated [arg] the oracle is
# the exact Array the real lodash.tail@4.1.1 returns (compared structurally:
# length + each element, with an own-`undefined` element encoded as {__t:undef}
# and distinguished from null). No seam.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SCOPE
# IN-CONTRACT LOGIC (reproduce exactly): the entire tail + inlined baseSlice
#   pipeline above, including the two falsy gates, the array-like .length
#   handling, the (end-1) >>> 0 / ToUint32 length truncation, dense fill of
#   missing positions, shallow element copy, fresh-Array result, and
#   non-mutation.
#
# CARRIED DATA: none. No embedded tables, no S-boxes, no charmaps.
#
# OUT-OF-CONTRACT / OUT-OF-ENVELOPE (documented, NOT emitted by genInputs
#   because they are not codec-round-trippable as persisted args, OR their
#   behavior depends on identity the codec drops). The behavior of each is
#   FULLY SPECIFIED above so a blind engineer can reconstruct it; they are
#   simply excluded from the GENERATED, graded oracle:
#
#     - SPARSE ARRAYS WITH HOLES  (e.g. tail([0,1,,3]) or tail([1,,3])):
#         BEHAVIOR (specified, see RESULT SHAPE (a)): the real tail densely
#         fills each hole that falls in the result range into an OWN `undefined`
#         property -> tail([0,1,,3]) === [1, undefined, 3] (all three own).
#         WHY EXCLUDED: a hole has NO JSON representation. The oracle persists
#         each input as JSON.stringify(encode(args)); the hole collapses to
#         `null`, so the input the blind re-emitter receives is the DENSE
#         [0,1,null,3] — a genuine null, not a hole. The stored `expected`
#         (computed on the LIVE sparse value before persist) is [1,undefined,3]
#         ({__t:undef} at index 1), but ANY correct emitter run on the revived
#         [0,1,null,3] computes [1,null,3]. undefined !== null, so expected can
#         NEVER match — the divergence is structural in JSON persistence, NOT an
#         emitter error. This is the SAME codec class as function/Symbol
#         elements below. (Confirmed: round-1 held-out v21/v22 split 0/3 for
#         exactly this reason; the fix is to NOT emit them.)
#         CONTRAST: an array-LIKE object with an absent numeric key
#         ({length:4,0:'a',1:'b'}) produces the same-shaped result but DOES
#         round-trip (absent own key survives JSON on both sides) and is KEPT
#         in-envelope — see RESULT SHAPE (b).
#
#     - FUNCTION elements (e.g. tail([1, ()=>2])): a function is shallow-copied
#       by reference exactly like any object element, but functions do not
#       survive JSON.stringify(encode(args)) round-trip, so they are NOT
#       generated. (Behavior, were it reconstructed: result[i] is the same
#       function reference.)
#     - SYMBOL elements (e.g. tail([1, Symbol()])): same — copied by reference,
#       but Symbols are not codec-faithful, so NOT generated. (Behavior: the
#       Symbol reference is shallow-copied.)
#     - Whole-argument identity types whose `.length` is absent are covered
#       in-envelope above (Map/Set -> []); only ELEMENT-position function/symbol
#       are excluded.
#   These are reconstructable by a blind engineer from this spec; they are
#   simply not in the generated input set.
#
#   NOTE on EXPLICIT-undefined ELEMENTS (IN-ENVELOPE, kept): an array with an
#   explicitly-assigned undefined element (e.g. [1, undefined, 3], an OWN
#   property whose value is undefined — NOT a hole) IS codec-faithful: the
#   encoder represents undefined as {__t:undef} and it round-trips. tail
#   preserves it as an own undefined. This case stays in the generated set and
#   must NOT be confused with the excluded sparse-hole case.

